# Reference and golden data are byte-exact artefacts. Their digests are
# recorded in a manifest and compared as bytes, and the topology goldens are
# compared character for character against generated output. Line-ending
# translation on checkout changes those bytes, so a Windows checkout made
# every recorded digest wrong -- measured, on a CI runner, which is how this
# file came to exist. `-text` disables the translation entirely.
tests/golden/** -text

# Shell and Perl keep LF whatever the platform. A CRLF script does not run on
# Linux, and the recipes are driven by them, so a commit from a Windows
# checkout would otherwise break every recipe silently.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.pl text eol=lf

# Kaldi config files likewise. Kaldi parses them as `--key=value` lines, so a
# trailing carriage return becomes part of the value -- `--dither=0` is not
# `--dither=0`. It also made the capture's copy of conf/fbank.conf disagree
# with the recipe's on a Windows checkout, which is how this was noticed:
# the configuration guard compares them by digest, and the reference side is
# `-text` while the recipe side was being translated.
*.conf text eol=lf
